{"title":"Ishika's Green Leaf Discovery","gradeSubjectTag":"Class 5 · Science · Plant Biology","readMinutes":2,"scenes":[{"label":"hook","text":"Ishika was helping her grandmother water the plants on the balcony one sunny morning. She picked up a leaf from the neem tree and held it up to the light. 'Nani, why are all these leaves so green?' she asked. Her grandmother smiled and said, 'That's a wonderful question. Let me show you why.'"},{"label":"problem","text":"Ishika's science project was due next week, and she had to answer: why are leaves green and not any other colour? She searched through books but felt confused by big words. She decided to ask her grandmother to explain it in a way she could understand and remember."},{"label":"mathMoment","text":"'Look at this leaf closely,' said her grandmother. 'Inside every leaf are tiny, tiny parts called chlorophyll. Chlorophyll is a green colour that catches sunlight. When sunlight falls on the leaf, chlorophyll catches the red and blue colours from that light and uses them to make food for the plant. But the green colour bounces back — it doesn't get caught. That's why we see green, not red or blue. The leaf is like a tiny factory, and chlorophyll is the worker that catches sunlight to help the plant grow and stay alive.'"},{"label":"wrapUp","text":"Ishika drew a picture of a leaf with chlorophyll inside it catching red and blue light, with green light bouncing away. When she presented her project at school, she explained it the way her grandmother had taught her. Her teacher was so pleased that she gave Ishika a gold star and said, 'You really understand how nature works!'"]}],"visual":null,"equations":[],"tryTogether":{"question":"You go to the park and see leaves on a mango tree, a tulsi plant, and moss on a rock. Why do you think they all look green, even though they grow in different places?","answer":"They all have chlorophyll inside them, which catches sunlight and makes green light bounce back to our eyes."},"parentPrompt":"Next time you water a plant together, hold a leaf up to the light and watch the green glow through it — talk about what's happening inside."}
